  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nModel uncertainty remains quite high for much of next week,\nespecially on Monday as at least the eastern half of Arizona\nshould retain good moisture, while an upper level low swings\nthrough Nevada during the first part of the day. We are still\nunsure how much instability will be present and if the low will\nmove to the north too quickly, but guidance is still favoring\n30-60% PoPs from Phoenix and areas to the east of Phoenix for \nmuch of Monday. Monday is likely to be very conditional on any\nstrong to severe storm threat due to the question of instability,\nbut we are expecting better wind shear on Monday. The passing\ntrough to our northwest should also begin to sweep drier air\nthrough a good portion of our area on Monday, but this may only\nimpact western areas during the first part of the day leaving \nenough moisture from Maricopa County eastward. This drying and \nthe eventual loss of upper level support is likely to mostly end \nrain chances on Tuesday before returning by next Wednesday or \nThursday. Guidance suggests the tropics across the eastern Pacific\nwill remain very active next week likely leading to more moisture\ngetting advected into the Desert Southwest during the latter half\nof next week. Forecast temperatures are shown returning into or \neven a few degrees below the normal range early next week before \nrising midweek as conditions temporarily dry out. The subtropical \nridge is also expected to nudge back into our region from the east\nhelping to boost temperatures back to or a few degrees above \nnormal at some point late next week.",
